static.solution:
\mathcal{D} is defined by the inequalities \mathcal{D}:\,2\le xy\le 6,\ 0\le x-y\le 5,\ x\ge 0,\ y\ge 0
With a_0=2,\quad a_1=6\quad and a_2=5
Since u=xy and v=x-y, the image of \mathcal{D} under this mapping is the rectangle defined by {\mathcal{D}}_0:\,2\le u\le 6,\ 0\le v\le 5 That is, {\mathcal{D}}_0=[2,6]\times [0,5].
\mathbf{(a)} We compute the Jacobian \frac{\partial ( u,v )}{\partial ( x,y )}
and then use the formula for the Jacobian of the inverse mapping to compute \frac{\partial ( x,y )}{\partial ( u,v )}.
Since u=xy and v=x-y, we have \frac{\partial ( u,v )}{\partial ( x,y )}= \left|\begin{array}{cc} \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}\amp \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\\ \noalign{\medskip} \frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\amp \frac{\partial v}{\partial y}\end{array}\right|=\left|\begin{array}{cc}y\amp x\\ 1\amp -1\end{array}\right|= -y-x= -( x+y ) Therefore, \frac{\partial ( x,y )}{\partial ( u,v )}={\left(\frac{\partial ( u,v )}{\partial ( x,y )}\right)}^{-1}=-\frac{1}{x+y}
\mathbf{(b)} In \mathcal{D}, x\ge 0 and y\ge 0, hence \left|\frac{\partial ( x,y )}{\partial ( u,v )}\right|=\frac{1}{x+y}.
Using the change of variable formula gives: \begin{array}{rcl} I= \int\!\!\int _{{\mathcal{D}}_0} ({x}^2-{y}^2)\cdot \frac{1}{x+y} \,du\,dv\amp =\amp \int\!\!\int _{{\mathcal{D}}_0} ( x-y ) \,du \,dv\\ \amp =\amp \int_0^{5} \int_{2}^{6} v \,du \,dv\\ \amp =\amp \left(\int_0^{5} v \,dv\right)\left(\int_{2}^{6}\,du\right)\\ \amp =\amp \left(\frac{{v}^2}{2}\bigg|_0^{5}\right)\left(u\bigg|_{2}^{6}\right)\\ \amp =\amp \frac{25}{2}\cdot 4=50 \end{array}
